.TH WAIT-UNTIL 1 "2026-10-13" "agentguard" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
wait-until \- wait for one concrete condition with a bounded deadline
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wait-until
.RB [ \-\-timeout
.IR secs ]
.RB [ \-\-interval
.IR secs ]
.B \-\-file
.I path
.br
.B wait-until
.RI [ options ]
.B \-\-pattern
.I regex
.B \-\-in
.I file
.br
.B wait-until
.RI [ options ]
.B \-\-pid
.I pid
.br
.B wait-until
.RI [ options ]
.B \-\-cmd
.I command
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wait-until
polls exactly one condition and exits as soon as it holds. It replaces a
fixed foreground
.B sleep
before checking a result: the wait ends when the state arrives instead of
after a guessed duration, and it fails with a diagnostic at the deadline
instead of running into a tool timeout.
.BR agent-hook-pre-bash (1)
points agents here when they run a long foreground
.BR sleep .
.PP
Options also accept the
.BI \-\- option = value
form.
.TP
.BI \-\-file " path"
The path exists.
.TP
.BI \-\-pattern " regex " \-\-in " file"
The file exists and contains a line matching the extended regular expression.
A missing file keeps waiting.
.TP
.BI \-\-pid " pid"
The process has exited. An exited but unreaped (zombie) process counts as
exited. Existence comes from
.BR "kill \-0" ,
which sees every pid even when a hidepid
.I /proc
or a missing
.BR ps (1)
would not: "no such process" means exited, "operation not permitted" means
another user's live process. Zombies, which still answer
.BR "kill \-0" ,
are spotted through
.I /proc
on Linux and
.B ps
elsewhere; without either, a zombie keeps the wait going rather than risk a
false exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-cmd " command"
.B bash \-c
.I command
exits 0. Each probe runs in its own process group with no standard input and
discarded output. Whatever is left of that group when the probe finishes, or
when the deadline passes with the probe still running, is stopped with TERM,
then KILL after a two-second grace period. A probe started at or near the
deadline, including the only probe of
.BR "\-\-timeout 0" ,
still gets two seconds to finish, so
.B \-\-cmd
can overrun the timeout by that much plus the grace period.
.BR agent-hook-pre-bash (1)
applies its command guards to the payload as it does to a
.B bash \-c
payload.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " secs"
Whole seconds to wait before giving up (default 90, under the 120-second
default Bash tool timeout of Claude Code). Bash 5 and later measure it in
milliseconds; older Bash (such as macOS /bin/bash 3.2) only counts whole
seconds, so there the wait can end up to a second early. With 0 the condition
is checked once. Longer waits need an explicit
.B \-\-timeout
and should run as a background tool call
.RB ( run_in_background )
so the agent's tool timeout cannot kill them first.
.TP
.BI \-\-interval " secs"
Seconds between checks (default 2); fractions such as 0.5 are accepted. The
last sleep is shortened so the final check lands on the deadline.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The condition held.
.TP
.B 124
The deadline passed first. One line on standard error names the condition and
its last observed state, such as
.BR missing ,
.BR "no match" ,
the process state, or the probe's last exit status.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.TP
.BR 129 ", " 130 ", " 143
Interrupted by HUP, INT, or TERM; a running probe is stopped first.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
wait-until \-\-timeout 300 \-\-pattern '^READY' \-\-in server.log
wait-until \-\-pid "$build_pid" \-\-timeout 900 \-\-interval 5  # in the background
wait-until \-\-cmd 'curl \-fs localhost:8080/health' \-\-timeout 60
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR agent-hook-pre-bash (1),
.BR sleep (1),
.BR timeout (1)
